NNAAMMEE
     dduummppffss — dump file system information

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     dduummppffss [--mm] _f_i_l_e_s_y_s | _d_e_v_i_c_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     dduummppffss prints out the super block and cylinder group information for the
     file system or special device specified, unless --mm is specified.  The
     listing is very long and detailed.

     dduummppffss is useful mostly for finding out certain file system information
     such as the file system block size, minimum free space percentage, and
     the file system level that can be upgraded with the --cc option of
     fsck_ffs(8).  All of this information can be found within the first
     twenty lines of the output.

     If --mm is specified, a newfs(8) command is output that can be used to gen‐
     erate a new file system with equivalent settings.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     disktab(5), fs(5), disklabel(8), fsck(8), fsck_ffs(8), growfs(8),
     newfs(8), tunefs(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The dduummppffss command appeared in 4.2BSD.

